★ MEMORY. `ldr`/`str` are the SCALED-offset forms and `ldur`/`stur` the UNSCALED ones — four spellings for four DSS opcodes, one each. ⚠ THIS IS WHY THEY ARE NOT ONE ROW WITH `["load", "load_u"]`: those two opcodes' variant guards are BYTE-IDENTICAL (`["reg", "membase", "memoffset"]` at each of widths 64/32/16/8), so `guard.operandKinds` election could not choose between them — it would take the first and silently encode LDR where the programmer wrote LDUR. gas distinguishes them by spelling, so the dialect does too, and the election facility is left to the case that actually needs it (AT&T's `movq`).
★★★ THESE FOUR ROWS WERE INVERTED WHEN THIS DIALECT SHIPPED (`e5b60f6c`) AND ARE CORRECTED HERE — D-ASM-ARM64-DIALECT-INVERTS-LDR-LDUR-MAPPING. The trap is the `_u` SUFFIX, and it is worth naming because the comment above was RIGHT about gas while the rows below contradicted it. `_u` in DSS's opcode names abbreviates **UNSIGNED-offset** (the scaled `[Xn, #pimm]` form), NOT **unscaled** — read as 'unscaled' it points at exactly the opposite instruction, which is what happened. ✔MEASURED against `arm64.target.json`'s own encodings, which are unambiguous:
  • DSS `load`  → fixedWord 0xF8400000, wire `imm9`         = **LDUR** Xt,[Xn,#simm9] — unscaled, SIGNED 9-bit;
  • DSS `store` → fixedWord 0xF8000000, wire `imm9`         = **STUR**;
  • DSS `load_u`→ fixedWord 0xF9400000, wire `imm12.scaled` = **LDR**  Xt,[Xn,#pimm]  — scaled, UNSIGNED 12-bit;
  • DSS `store_u`→fixedWord 0xF9000000, wire `imm12.scaled` = **STR**.
So gas `ldr`/`str` (scaled, unsigned) are DSS `load_u`/`store_u`, and gas `ldur`/`stur` (unscaled, signed) are DSS `load`/`store`. ⚠ THE OBSERVABLE COST OF THE INVERSION WAS A FAIL-LOUD, NOT A MISCOMPILE, WHICH IS THE ONLY REASON IT SURVIVED A CYCLE: `stur x1, [x0, #-8]` elected the SCALED opcode, whose imm12 field is unsigned, and the encoder refused the negative offset (`A_ImmediateOperandOutOfRange`). The reverse direction was the dangerous one — a `ldr x0,[x0,#8]` reaching the UNSCALED encoder produced a real word with the offset read as a raw byte count rather than a scaled one, i.e. the right instruction shape at the wrong address for every offset that is not a multiple of the access size.
